# Packages-Live — the LIVE-ONLY layer of the Runink River ISO (profile.yaml
# `livefs:`, LiveOS/livefs.img). Never in rootfs.img; 20-clone-rootfs removes them from an
# installed machine (the install clones the running live system, which includes this layer).
# river lint profile-manifest checks that profile.yaml's livefs list is exactly this file.
#
# No artix-live-s6 / artix-live-base: the live session is configuration in live-overlay/
# (see the header of profile.yaml).

## The live GRUB scaffolding artools copies onto the ISO: the /usr/share/grub paths
## prepare_grub() reads out of THIS layer (cfg/*.cfg, locales/, tz/). OURS, built from
## build/pkgbuilds/runink-grub-live; it replaced Artix's artix-grub-live, whose MENU this
## image never used (iso-profiles/river/grub/ is the menu — see scripts/patch-artools.go).
## The PKGBUILD header lists, path by path, what artools reads and why each one is there.
runink-grub-live

## cage — a Wayland compositor that runs exactly one fullscreen application.
##
## WHY IT IS HERE, and why it is not wpewebkit any more. river-live-fallback's chain is: a
## graphical session, else river-kiosk, else the text installer. The kiosk used to be WPE
## WebKit's MiniBrowser drawn straight onto KMS/DRM with no compositor at all. That wants a
## hardware cursor plane and a GPU renderer, and a plain EFI framebuffer (simpledrm) has
## neither -- so it failed on precisely the machines that needed a fallback. On the owner's
## XPS 13 9300 it aborted five times in ten seconds with "No suitable DRM device found", on a
## laptop where CachyOS, Manjaro and Fedora all open their installer without trouble.
##
## Those distributions do the boring thing: boot a session, run the installer as an ordinary
## window in it. So does this now. cage drives KMS through wlroots -- which is a compositor's
## actual job -- and falls back to software rendering (WLR_RENDERER=pixman) where there is no
## GPU to render with. Inside it runs the SAME river-installer-desktop the Plasma session runs,
## so there is one browser, one launcher and one definition of what the installer looks like.
##
## firefox is already in Packages-Root, so this swap ADDS a small compositor and REMOVES a
## whole second browser engine.
##
## It is LIVE-ONLY on purpose. An installed workstation has Plasma and a browser and no use for
## the kiosk; 20-clone-rootfs removes this layer from the installed machine, so the installed
## footprint is unchanged. The cost is paid only on the medium that needs a graphical installer
## without a desktop.
##
## Downstream images are unaffected: a profile that needs a different kiosk ships its own copy
## of river-kiosk and its own live package list, because an image with no desktop should not
## grow one just to show an installer.
cage
